      "question": "I've heard the ratio method can give odd answers when gas levels are low, so I want a second opinion on Transformer 1 at site MAIN. Using the average hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene over the available window, run the IEC 60599 ratio classification. Separately, work out the Duval Triangle 1 percentages from methane, ethylene and acetylene, and name the zone they fall in (PD, D1, D2, DT, T1, T2 or T3). Tell me whether the two methods agree. If they disagree, tell me which one you trust more and why, looking at whether the gases are high enough for ratios to mean anything. Then name the single most likely failure mode from our known transformer list. Use averages, not single samples. If the combined methane, ethylene and acetylene is under 10 ppm, say that the Duval result isn't meaningful and rely on the ratio result alone. If methane or ethylene has no data at all, say that neither method can be applied reliably and give a key-gas view from the largest gas instead.",
      "answer": "I ran both methods on the 5-sample averages for Transformer 1 (15\u201319 Jan 2024). The IEC 60599 ratio tool returned \"LLM unavailable\", so I calculated the ratio classification myself.\n\n**Average gases (ppm):** H\u2082 2,869 \u00b7 CH\u2084 7,331.2 \u00b7 C\u2082H\u2086 5,457.4 \u00b7 C\u2082H\u2084 16,626.8 \u00b7 C\u2082H\u2082 7.0\n\n**IEC 60599 ratio method**\n| Ratio | Value | Code |\n|---|---|---|\n| C\u2082H\u2082/C\u2082H\u2084 | 0.0004 | 0 (below 0.1) |\n| CH\u2084/H\u2082 | 2.56 | 2 (above 1) |\n| C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 | 3.05 | 2 (above 3) |\n\n- **Code 0-2-2: thermal fault above 700 \u00b0C (T3).**\n- The C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 ratio of 3.05 is only just over the cut-off. The newer IEC 60599 table puts T3 at a ratio above 4, and under that table this sample would read T2 (300\u2013700 \u00b0C). So the ratio method points to a high-temperature thermal fault, but whether it's T2 or T3 depends on which table you use.\n\n**Duval Triangle 1**\n- CH\u2084 + C\u2082H\u2084 + C\u2082H\u2082 = 23,965 ppm. That's far above the 10 ppm floor, and every gas has data, so no fallback rule applies.\n- %CH\u2084 = 30.6%, %C\u2082H\u2084 = 69.4%, %C\u2082H\u2082 = 0.03%\n- **Zone T3** (thermal fault above 700 \u00b0C): ethylene is over 50% and acetylene is under 15%.\n\n**Do they agree?** Yes. Both methods call it a severe thermal fault, and T3 on the classic IEC code. Since they agree, you don't need to pick one. Your worry about ratios at low gas levels doesn't apply here: every gas in the ratios is in the thousands of ppm, except acetylene. Acetylene is low at 7 ppm, but that's what a thermal fault looks like, as opposed to arcing. The ratios are well supported. The only doubt is where the C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 ratio sits against the table boundary. Duval doesn't have that problem, so I'd lean on Duval for the T3 call.\n\nThe unit's own readings back this up. The oil temperature alarm and trip flags are both set, and CO and CO\u2082 are fairly modest (about 32 and 1,332 ppm). That suggests the heat is mainly in the oil and metal rather than breaking down the paper insulation.\n\n**Most likely failure mode from your transformer list: Thermal Fault \u2014 Core/Winding.** Faults above 700 \u00b0C with lots of ethylene usually come from hot metal, such as circulating currents in the core, bad joints, or winding hot spots, rather than the oil simply running hot.",
      "score": {
